<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Friedenstein Palace</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1643–1654</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Schloss Friedenstein</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Friedenstein Palace is the residence built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, between 1643 and 1654 by Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-i-duke-of-saxe-gotha">Ernest the Pious</a> of Saxe-Gotha. Its name, meaning &quot;rock of peace&quot;, reflects hopes for the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years' War. One of the largest early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> palaces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, it contains the Ekhof <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> with its original stage machinery and the ducal collections of art, coins and natural history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/friedenstein-palace.jpg" alt="Friedenstein Palace" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Selby · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schloss-Friedenstein01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
